  "narrative_body": "At 3:49 on the morning of May 15, Finland's Interior Ministry issued an emergency warning across Uusimaa — the greater Helsinki region, home to 1.8 million people — after intelligence suggested Ukrainian drones carrying heavy explosive payloads might stray into Finnish airspace. It was the clearest demonstration yet that the operational effects of the Ukraine-Russia war reach into NATO's northernmost capital.\n\nHelsinki-Vantaa airport was closed. Antti Häkkänen, the defence minister, authorized use of force. Petteri Orpo, the prime minister, was recalled from his cottage. By 7:06 AM the all-clear was issued, and Timo Herranen, Finland's air force commander, confirmed at a press conference that no drone had entered Finnish territory. The aftermath brought a complicating detail: the Finnish Defence Forces later confirmed the drones had approached from the Russian direction, not from Ukrainian airspace — meaning they had already passed through or over Russian territory before nearing the Finnish border, a finding that leaves the full incident geometry unresolved.\n\nFinland has now twice formally asserted airspace sovereignty against Ukrainian drone spillover. Alexander Stubb, the president, called Volodymyr Zelensky overnight. Mr Häkkänen stated publicly that if Ukrainian drone operations endanger NATO member states, \"it is condemnable and wrong every time.\" The dual-track Ukraine relationship — deepen the defence partnership, enforce sovereignty — has moved from posture to operational fact.\n\nThe incident also exposed a gap in Finland's civil protection system. The rescue chief initially told reporters that at least one drone may have entered Finnish territory, then retracted the claim as a miscommunication. Citizens had no SMS emergency alert — Finland's EU-alert system will not be available until 2027 — and were reportedly uncertain whether to send children to school. Mr Orpo acknowledged the failure: \"People need to have clear information